  "description": "O3' selects a block, but NOT the bound state's block. The objective t_lambda = w_lambda * chi^lambda(W_atom) factors into an atom-INDEPENDENT weight and an atom-dependent character; measured over all six committed rows, argmin(t) == argmin(w) every time, so the character never moves the selection. Two atoms at n=3 and two at n=9 give the SAME block with DIFFERENT prices. Mechanism UNEXPLAINED: the first spread-dominance account is refuted here -- chi-spread exceeds w-spread at n=9 and the argmin is unmoved anyway. THE MECHANISM THAT DOES hold is an ANTI-CORRELATION between w and chi: concordant partition pairs are 9/49 = 18% against 24.5 expected, so a large character lands preferentially on a small weight and the product is FLATTENED relative to w on every row (never amplified) -- chi is a partial cancellation of the ordering it would have to reverse, not an independent perturbation. Exact distribution-free tail, one row per distinct n and the least extreme within each: 343/518400 ~ 6.6e-4. Cancellation is INCOMPLETE (t-spread 1.35x-10.2x), so w's argmin survives. WHY the anti-correlation holds REDUCES to the content sum: w is a strictly increasing function of c(lambda) on 49/49 pairs, so conc(w,|chi|) == conc(c(lambda),|chi|) IDENTICALLY row by row, and the effect decomposes as c up => w up (49/49 exact, 0 ties), c up => dim down (6/48), dim up => |chi| up (43/48 = 89.6%). For two-row partitions [n] has max content and dim 1 while the balanced partition has min content and max dim. CORRECTED here: those last two denominators read 49 and n=6 was called 'uncorrelated at 3/6' -- a tie ([5,1] and [3,3] both have f^lambda = 5 at n=6, the only tie in the sample) was being counted as discordant; n=6 is 3/5 = 60%, weakly POSITIVE. The two exact claims are untouched because c(lambda), w and |chi| are tie-free, now asserted. SETTLED NEGATIVELY: the dim->|chi| link is STATISTICAL, not structural -- rows 3 and 4 are two atoms at n=9 with the identical dimension multiset {1,8,27,42,48} and disagree (10/10 vs 9/10), which no partition-only law could do. The 5 discordant pairs sit on three partitions: [4,2] at n=6, [7,2] at n=9, [7,5] at n=12. CONSEQUENCE: PR #6752's reading of the selection as 'which Wedderburn blocks the bound state saturates' is WRONG and is corrected; the pricing result (y_0* nonzero and atom-dependent, where the full LP has y_0* = 0 identically) STANDS.",
